Subject: [PATCH 0/3] DSF muxer and PCM to DSD encoding via libdsd_upsample

This series fixes a channel layout in the DSF demuxer, and adds a DSF (DSD
Stream File) muxer and an encoder wrapper for libdsd_upsample, a library
that converts PCM to DSD64, DSD128 or DSD256 with a delta-sigma modulator
(MIT OR Apache-2.0).

1/3 DSF demuxer fix. The DSF specification defines channel type 5
("4 channels") as front left, front right, center and low frequency (3.1);
the demuxer read it as 4.0.

2/3 DSF muxer. Accepts dsd_lsbf, dsd_msbf and their planar variants, 1 to 6
channels. Writes 4096-byte blocks per channel, least significant bit first
(bits per sample 1), zero-pads the last block and fixes up the sizes in the
trailer. Global metadata and chapters go into an ID3v2 tag after the data.
Channel types follow the demuxer's table, so files round-trip. The FATE tests
use the generated asynth WAV as raw DSD data, so no new samples are needed.

3/3 libdsdupsample encoder. Takes dblp at 44.1, 88.2, 176.4 or 352.8 kHz, or
at 48, 96, 192 or 384 kHz (the library resamples these by 147/160 to the
44.1 kHz family), and outputs dsd_msbf_planar. As for the other DSD codecs,
the output sample rate is the DSD bit rate / 8, which differs from the input
rate; the encoder sets avctx->sample_rate to the DSD rate in init, after the
input rate has been validated, and timestamps stay in the caller's time
base. I found no existing encoder that changes its rate. If an audio filter
producing AV_SAMPLE_FMT_DSD, encoded by the existing dsd_msbf encoder, is
preferred, I can rework it that way.

Tested with fate-dsf and fate-source (static and shared builds), and by
comparing the encoder's DSF output byte for byte with the library's own
command-line converter of the same library version (DSD64/128/256, several
orders, gains and channel counts, 44.1 to 352.8 and 48 to 384 kHz input,
with -fflags +bitexact -map_metadata -1 so that no ID3v2 tag is written).
The files are identical with two exceptions, both in the DSF header:
- When the converted audio ends inside a byte (common with 48 kHz-family
  input), the DSF header's sample count is rounded up to a whole byte,
  because FFmpeg counts DSD in bytes; the audio data is identical to the
  command-line tool's.
- For 4-channel input with the 3.1 layout, the muxer writes channel type 5
  (3.1, as patch 1 reads it) where the converter writes type 4 (quad).

aclater (3):
  avformat/dsfdec: fix the channel layout of channel type 5
  avformat: add DSF muxer
  avcodec: add PCM to DSD encoding via libdsd_upsample

